  "step_3": "The decision reason, as defined, relies on identifying the most favorable outcome with the minimum probability — but in this case, the least probable outcomes are both losses (unfavorable), so no such favorable rare outcome exists. The individual does not refer to rare or least probable outcomes; instead, they focus on the magnitude of the loss and judge the probabilities of losing as trivially different. There is no indication that they isolated the least probable outcome or evaluated it for favorability. Their reasoning is based on minimizing loss magnitude under roughly equal loss probabilities — a form of minimax or loss-aversion heuristic — not on analyzing rare events. The information used by the individual (loss size and approximate loss probability) does not match the information required by the decision reason (least probable outcome and its value). The justification (avoid larger loss when probabilities are similar) is unrelated to the reason’s rule (prefer lottery with more favorable least probable outcome). Therefore, it is certain that the individual did not use this decision reason.",
